 ## Overview
-The N³-tree with Brick Pool and Node Pool is a hierarchical data structure for voxel rendering that combines an N³-tree for spatial subdivision with a brick pool for volume data and a node pool for tree structure, allowing efficient LOD and visibility culling [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering large volumetric models by adapting the spatial resolution to the required precision, using small 3D texture units (bricks) that can be efficiently evaluated through ray marching [S1, unofficial]. What sets it apart from its siblings, such as [Brick Caching with LRU Replacement](879-brick-caching-with-lru-replacement.md) or [Brick-based Volume Raycasting](3341-brick-based-volume-raycasting.md), is its use of a single pointer per node stored in a 3D texture node pool, which reduces memory requirements by 16x compared to methods requiring eight child pointers and neighbor pointers [S1, unofficial]. This design improves cache coherence during traversal and minimizes the amount of data read during tree traversal [S1, unofficial].
+The N³-tree with Brick Pool and Node Pool is a hierarchical data structure for voxel rendering that combines an N³-tree for spatial subdivision with a brick pool for volume data and a node pool for tree structure, allowing efficient level-of-detail (LOD) and visibility culling [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering large volumetric models by adapting the spatial resolution to the required precision, using small 3D texture units (bricks) that can be efficiently evaluated through ray marching [S1, unofficial]. What sets it apart from its siblings, such as [Bricked Volume Hierarchy](5036-bricked-volume-hierarchy.md) or [Brick Caching with LRU Replacement](879-brick-caching-with-lru-replacement.md), is its use of a single pointer per node stored in a 3D texture node pool, which reduces memory requirements by 16x compared to methods requiring eight child pointers and neighbor pointers [S1, unofficial]. This design improves cache coherence during traversal and minimizes the amount of data read from the structure [S1, unofficial].
 
